  "publishedAt": "2026-11-17T05:05:00.000Z",
  "content": "<div id=\"readability-page-1\" class=\"page\"><div id=\"\"> <p>APOD</p> <p>Astronomy Picture of the Day</p> <p> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a> Each day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is featured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer. </p> <div><p><img width=\"1280\" height=\"1280\" src=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GalaxyVsAurora_Scheib_960.jpg/jcr:content/renditions/cq5dam.web.1280.1280.jpeg\" alt=\"A starry night sky is seen above water and some islands. In the night sky are two bright arches that are nearly vertical. On the left is the textured Milky Way and on the right is a smoother ribbon of aurora. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\" decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GalaxyVsAurora_Scheib_960.jpg/jcr:content/renditions/cq5dam.web.1280.1280.jpeg 1280w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GalaxyVsAurora_Scheib_960.jpg/jcr:content/renditions/cq5dam.thumbnail.319.319.png 319w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1280px) 100vw, 1280px\"><img width=\"1280\" height=\"1280\" src=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GalaxyVsAurora_Scheib_960_annotated.jpg/jcr:content/renditions/cq5dam.web.1280.1280.jpeg\" alt=\"A starry night sky is seen above water and some islands. In the night sky are two bright arches that are nearly vertical. On the left is the textured Milky Way and on the right is a smoother ribbon of aurora. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GalaxyVsAurora_Scheib_960_annotated.jpg/jcr:content/renditions/cq5dam.web.1280.1280.jpeg 1280w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GalaxyVsAurora_Scheib_960_annotated.jpg/jcr:content/renditions/cq5dam.thumbnail.319.319.png 319w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1280px) 100vw, 1280px\"></p></div> <div> <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Are these two giant arches battling for control of the sky? No, but it sure looks like it! The arch on the left is the central band of our&nbsp;<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-milky-way-galaxy/\">Milky Way Galaxy</a>, an ancient system of stars, gas, and dust that predates&nbsp;<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/planet-earth-through-the-ages/\">our Earth</a>&nbsp;and is very far away. The arch on the right, by contrast, is an&nbsp;<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2004-november-12-missouris-green-ribbon-sky/\">auroral ribbon</a>&nbsp;that is only hours old, composed of energized&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.bbc.co.uk/bitesize/articles/zc86m39#zygjdp3\">atoms and molecules</a>, and is close by -- right in&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/properties-of-earths-upper-atmosphere/\">Earth's upper atmosphere</a>. These chance-superposed&nbsp;<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-november-30-the-surface-of-titan-from-huygens/\">titan</a>s have&nbsp;<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-june-15-prominences-and-filaments-on-the-active-sun/\">our Sun</a>&nbsp;in common, a&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/media?url=https%3A%2F%2Fpreview.redd.it%2Fthis-is-the-most-cute-thing-i-ever-seen-v0-mpx697lakcue1.jpg%3Fwidth%3D1080%26crop%3Dsmart%26auto%3Dwebp%26s%3Da5d18b145135737912b5caa61fcc56766726f6cb\">stellar member</a>&nbsp;of the&nbsp;Milky Way&nbsp;that ejects energetic particles which create&nbsp;<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-february-10-in-green-company-aurora-over-norway/\">aurora</a>s. Far behind the middle of the&nbsp;<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-january-13-arches-across-an-arctic-sky/\">auroral arch</a>&nbsp;is the&nbsp;<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2013-april-21-the-big-dipper/\">Big Dipper</a>. Even farther in the distance is the&nbsp;<a href=\"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/11675/\">Andromeda galaxy</a>&nbsp;-- the bright spot near the top of the frame, just left of center. The&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DfAZEgmIDIq/\">featured image</a>&nbsp;was taken about a month ago from&nbsp;<a href=\"https://youtu.be/4VlzJh3WBGU\">Kvaløya</a>&nbsp;in northern&nbsp;<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norway\">Norway</a>.</p><p><strong>Tomorrow's picture:&nbsp;</strong>open space </p></div> </div></div>",
